00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,904
Can you show me the container's writable layer?

2
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:10,002
An image is read-only.

3
00:00:10,002 --> 00:00:16,143
When a container starts, Docker adds a thin writable layer on top of the image's layers:

4
00:00:16,143 --> 00:00:22,214
every file the container creates, changes or deletes is recorded there (copy-on-write).

5
00:00:22,214 --> 00:00:24,726
That layer belongs to the container:

6
00:00:24,726 --> 00:00:29,681
it survives a stop and a restart, and it is deleted with the container.

7
