28
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:57,831
What do I need to remember?

29
00:01:58,367 --> 00:02:04,231
Each container has a writable layer on top of the read-only image layers (copy-on-write).

30
00:02:04,767 --> 00:02:09,169
It survives stop and start, and is deleted with the container:

31
00:02:09,169 --> 00:02:11,441
never keep important data there.

32
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,954
docker diff shows what a container changed; docker container ls -s shows how much.

33
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:22,759
I keep hearing about bind mounts.

34
00:02:22,759 --> 00:02:24,519
What is it, really?

35
00:02:25,067 --> 00:02:30,712
A bind mount makes a file or folder of the host appear at a path inside the container.

36
00:02:30,712 --> 00:02:32,681
Both sides see the same files:

37
00:02:32,681 --> 00:02:36,686
change one on the host and the container sees it immediately.

38
00:02:36,686 --> 00:02:41,543
That makes bind mounts the tool for development (edit code on your laptop,

39
00:02:41,543 --> 00:02:46,401
run it in a container) and for handing configuration files to a container.

