56
00:03:48,247 --> 00:03:50,998
What do I need to remember?

57
00:03:51,533 --> 00:03:58,488
A bind mount shares a host file or folder with the container; changes are visible on both sides at once.

58
00:03:59,033 --> 00:04:06,553
The host path must be absolute ("$(pwd)/…"); a bare name creates a named volume instead.

59
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:14,000
Add :ro (or readonly) unless the container must write; a mount hides the image's files at the target path.

60
00:04:16,733 --> 00:04:19,203
How does named volumes work?

61
00:04:19,767 --> 00:04:26,367
A named volume is storage that Docker creates and manages, independent of any container.

62
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:29,666
You refer to it by name (-v app-data:/data),

63
00:04:29,666 --> 00:04:35,291
several containers can use it, and it survives when containers are removed.

64
00:04:35,291 --> 00:04:40,166
Volumes are the default choice for data that an application owns:

65
00:04:40,166 --> 00:04:42,191
databases, uploads, queues.

