81
00:05:41,913 --> 00:05:44,665
What do I need to remember?

82
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:51,905
Named volumes are managed by Docker, independent of containers, and survive docker rm.

83
00:05:52,467 --> 00:05:57,476
Several containers can mount the same volume (use :ro for readers).

84
00:05:58,033 --> 00:06:03,915
A volume in use by any container, even a stopped one, cannot be deleted:

85
00:06:03,915 --> 00:06:06,693
docker ps -a --filter volume=NAME.

86
00:06:08,433 --> 00:06:11,188
Why would I need inspecting volumes?

87
00:06:11,733 --> 00:06:16,564
Volumes outlive containers, so they pile up and need looking after:

88
00:06:16,564 --> 00:06:20,241
which volumes exist, which container uses each one,

89
00:06:20,241 --> 00:06:26,081
where the data is, how big it is, and which ones nobody uses any more (dangling).

90
00:06:26,081 --> 00:06:29,253
Some volumes have no meaningful name at all:

91
00:06:29,253 --> 00:06:40,068
an image that declares VOLUME /path gets an anonymous volume (a random 64-character name) every time a container starts without a mount for that path.

92
00:06:40,068 --> 00:06:44,178
Anonymous volumes are where "lost" data is usually found.

